|             The tick labels of the bars.\n2702 |             Default: None (Use default numeric labels.)\n2703 | \n2704 |         label : str or list of str, optional\n2705 |             A single label is attached to the resulting `.BarContainer` as a\n2706 |             label for the whole dataset.\n2707 |             If a list is provided, it must be the same length as *y* and\n2708 |             labels the individual bars. Repeated labels are not de-duplicated\n2709 |             and will cause repeated label entries, so this is best used when\n2710 |             bars also differ in style (e.g., by passing a list to *color*.)\n2711 | \n2712 |         xerr, yerr : float or array-like of shape(N,) or shape(2, N), optional\n2713 |             If not *None*, add horizontal / vertical errorbars to the bar tips.\n2714 |             The values are +/- sizes relative to the data:\n2715 | \n2716 |             - scalar: symmetric +/- values for all bars\n2717 |             - shape(N,): symmetric +/- values for each bar\n2718 |             - shape(2, N): Separate - and + values for each bar. First row\n2719 |               contains the lower errors, the second row contains the upper\n2720 |               errors.\n2721 |             - *None*: No errorbar. (default)\n2722 | \n2723 |             See :doc:`/gallery/statistics/errorbar_features` for an example on\n2724 |             the usage of *xerr* and *yerr*.\n2725 | \n2726 |         ecolor : :mpltype:`color` or list of :mpltype:`color`, default: 'black'\n2727 |             The line color of the errorbars.\n2728 | \n2729 |         capsize : float, default: :rc:`errorbar.capsize`\n2730 |            The length of the error bar caps in points.\n2731 | \n2732 |         error_kw : dict, optional\n2733 |             Dictionary of keyword arguments to be passed to the\n2734 |             `~.Axes.errorbar` method. Values of *ecolor* or *capsize* defined\n2735 |             here take precedence over the independent keyword arguments.\n2736 | \n2737 |         log : bool, default: False\n2738 |             If ``True``, set the x-axis to be log scale.\n2739 | \n2740 |         data : indexable object, optional\n2741 |             If given, all parameters also accept a string ``s``, which is\n2742 |             interpreted as ``data[s]`` if  ``s`` is a key in ``data``.\n2743 | \n2744 |         **kwargs : `.Rectangle` properties\n2745 | \n2746 |         %(Rectangle:kwdoc)s\n2747 | \n2748 |         See Also\n2749 |         --------\n2750 |         bar : Plot a vertical bar plot.\n2751 | \n2752 |         Notes\n2753 |         -----\n2754 |         Stacked bars can be achieved by passing individual *left* values per\n2755 |         bar. See\n2756 |         :doc:`/gallery/lines_bars_and_markers/horizontal_barchart_distribution`.\n2757 |         \"\"\"\n2758 |         kwargs.setdefault('orientation', 'horizontal')\n2759 |         patches = self.bar(x=left, height=height, width=width, bottom=y,\n2760 |                            align=align, data=data, **kwargs)\n2761 |         return patches\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"def bar(\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `lib/matplotlib/axes/_axes.py:Axes.bar_label`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n2763 | def bar_label(self, container, labels=None, *, fmt=\"%g\", label_type=\"edge\",\n2764 |                   padding=0, **kwargs):\n2765 |         \"\"\"\n2766 |         Label a bar plot.\n2767 | \n2768 |         Adds labels to bars in the given `.BarContainer`.\n2769 |         You may need to adjust the axis limits to fit the labels.\n2770 | \n2771 |         Parameters\n2772 |         ----------\n2773 |         container : `.BarContainer`\n2774 |             Container with all the bars and optionally errorbars, likely\n2775 |             returned from `.bar` or `.barh`.\n2776 | \n2777 |         labels : array-like, optional\n2778 |             A list of label texts, that should be displayed. If not given, the\n2779 |             label texts will be the data values formatted with *fmt*.\n2780 | \n2781 |         fmt : str or callable, default: '%g'\n2782 |             An unnamed %-style or {}-style format string for the label or a\n2783 |             function to call with the value as the first argument.\n2784 |             When *fmt* is a string and can be interpreted in both formats,\n2785 |             %-style takes precedence over {}-style.\n2786 | \n2787 |             .. versionadded:: 3.7\n2788 |                Support for {}-style format string and callables.\n2789 | \n2790 |         label_type : {'edge', 'center'}, default: 'edge'\n2791 |             The label type. Possible values:\n2792 | \n2793 |             - 'edge': label placed at the end-point of the bar segment, and the\n2794 |               value displayed will be the position of that end-point.\n2795 |             - 'center': label placed in the center of the bar segment, and the\n2796 |               value displayed will be the length of that segment.\n2797 |               (useful for stacked bars, i.e.,\n2798 |               :doc:`/gallery/lines_bars_and_markers/bar_label_demo`)\n2799 | \n2800 |         padding : float, default: 0\n2801 |             Distance of label from the end of the bar, in points.\n2802 | \n2803 |         **kwargs\n2804 |             Any remaining keyword arguments are passed through to\n2805 |             `.Axes.annotate`. The alignment parameters (\n2806 |             *horizontalalignment* / *ha*, *verticalalignment* / *va*) are\n2807 |             not supported because the labels are automatically aligned to\n2808 |             the bars.\n2809 | \n2810 |         Returns\n2811 |         -------\n2812 |         list of `.Annotation`\n2813 |             A list of `.Annotation` instances for the labels.\n2814 |         \"\"\"\n2815 |         for key in ['horizontalalignment', 'ha', 'verticalalignment', 'va']:\n2816 |             if key in kwargs:\n2817 |                 raise ValueError(\n2818 |                     f\"Passing {key!r} to bar_label() is not supported.\")\n2819 | \n2820 |         a, b = self.yaxis.get_view_interval()\n2821 |         y_inverted = a > b\n2822 |         c, d = self.xaxis.get_view_interval()\n2823 |         x_inverted = c > d\n2824 | \n2825 |         # want to know whether to put label on positive or negative direction\n2826 |         # cannot use np.sign here because it will return 0 if x == 0\n2827 |         def sign(x):\n2828 |             return 1 if x >= 0 else -1\n2829 | \n2830 |         _api.check_in_list(['edge', 'center'], label_type=label_type)\n2831 | \n2832 |         bars = container.patches\n2833 |         errorbar = container.errorbar\n2834 |         datavalues = container.datavalues\n2835 |         orientation = container.orientation\n2836 | \n2837 |         if errorbar:\n2838 |             # check \"ErrorbarContainer\" for the definition of these elements\n2839 |             lines = errorbar.lines  # attribute of \"ErrorbarContainer\" (tuple)\n2840 |             barlinecols = lines[2]  # 0: data_line, 1: caplines, 2: barlinecols\n2841 |             barlinecol = barlinecols[0]  # the \"LineCollection\" of error bars\n2842 |             errs = barlinecol.get_segments()\n2843 |         else:\n2844 |             errs = []\n2845 | \n2846 |         if labels is None:\n2847 |             labels = []\n2848 | \n2849 |         annotations = []\n2850 | \n2851 |         for bar, err, dat, lbl in itertools.zip_longest(\n2852 |                 bars, errs, datavalues, labels\n2853 |         ):\n2854 |             (x0, y0), (x1, y1) = bar.get_bbox().get_points()\n2855 |             xc, yc = (x0 + x1) / 2, (y0 + y1) / 2\n2856 | \n2857 |             if orientation == \"vertical\":\n2858 |                 extrema = max(y0, y1) if dat >= 0 else min(y0, y1)\n2859 |                 length = abs(y0 - y1)\n2860 |             else:  # horizontal\n2861 |                 extrema = max(x0, x1) if dat >= 0 else min(x0, x1)\n2862 |                 length = abs(x0 - x1)\n2863 | \n2864 |             if err is None or np.size(err) == 0:\n2865 |                 endpt = extrema\n2866 |             elif orientation == \"vertical\":\n2867 |                 endpt = err[:, 1].max() if dat >= 0 else err[:, 1].min()\n2868 |             else:  # horizontal\n2869 |                 endpt = err[:, 0].max() if dat >= 0 else err[:, 0].min()\n2870 | \n2871 |             if label_type == \"center\":\n2872 |                 value = sign(dat) * length\n2873 |             else:  # edge\n2874 |                 value = extrema\n2875 | \n2876 |             if label_type == \"center\":\n2877 |                 xy = (0.5, 0.5)\n2878 |                 kwargs[\"xycoords\"] = (\n2879 |                     lambda r, b=bar:\n2880 |                         mtransforms.Bbox.intersection(\n2881 |                             b.get_window_extent(r), b.get_clip_box()\n2882 |                         ) or mtransforms.Bbox.null()\n2883 |                 )\n2884 |             else:  # edge\n2885 |                 if orientation == \"vertical\":\n2886 |                     xy = xc, endpt\n2887 |                 else:  # horizontal\n2888 |                     xy = endpt, yc\n2889 | \n2890 |             if orientation == \"vertical\":\n2891 |                 y_direction = -1 if y_inverted else 1\n2892 |                 xytext = 0, y_direction * sign(dat) * padding\n2893 |             else:  # horizontal\n2894 |                 x_direction = -1 if x_inverted else 1\n2895 |                 xytext = x_direction * sign(dat) * padding, 0\n2896 | \n2897 |             if label_type == \"center\":\n2898 |                 ha, va = \"center\", \"center\"\n2899 |             else:  # edge\n2900 |                 if orientation == \"vertical\":\n2901 |                     ha = 'center'\n2902 |                     if y_inverted:\n2903 |                         va = 'top' if dat > 0 else 'bottom'  # also handles NaN\n2904 |                     else:\n2905 |                         va = 'top' if dat < 0 else 'bottom'  # also handles NaN\n2906 |                 else:  # horizontal\n2907 |                     if x_inverted:\n2908 |                         ha = 'right' if dat > 0 else 'left'  # also handles NaN\n2909 |                     else:\n2910 |                         ha = 'right' if dat < 0 else 'left'  # also handles NaN\n2911 |                     va = 'center'\n2912 | \n2913 |             if np.isnan(dat):\n2914 |                 lbl = ''\n2915 | \n2916 |             if lbl is None:\n2917 |                 if isinstance(fmt, str):\n2918 |                     lbl = cbook._auto_format_str(fmt, value)\n2919 |                 elif callable(fmt):\n2920 |                     lbl = fmt(value)\n2921 |                 else:\n2922 |                     raise TypeError(\"fmt must be a str or callable\")\n2923 |             annotation = self.annotate(lbl,\n2924 |                                        xy, xytext, textcoords=\"offset points\",\n2925 |                                        ha=ha, va=va, **kwargs)\n2926 |             annotations.append(annotation)\n2927 | \n2928 |         return annotations\n```\n\nFound function `lib/matplotlib/axes/_axes.py:Axes.hexbin`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n4981 | def hexbin(self, x, y, C=None, gridsize=100, bins=None,\n4982 |                xscale='linear', yscale='linear', extent=None,\n4983 |                cmap=None, norm=None, vmin=None, vmax=None,\n4984 |                alpha=None, linewidths=None, edgecolors='face',\n4985 |                reduce_C_function=np.mean, mincnt=None, marginals=False,\n4986 |                colorizer=None, **kwargs):\n4987 |         \"\"\"\n4988 |         Make a 2D hexagonal binning plot of points *x*, *y*.\n4989 | \n4990 |         If *C* is *None*, the value of the hexagon is determined by the number\n4991 |         of points in the hexagon. Otherwise, *C* specifies values at the\n4992 |         coordinate (x[i], y[i]). For each hexagon, these values are reduced\n4993 |         using *reduce_C_function*.\n4994 | \n4995 |         Parameters\n4996 |         ----------\n4997 |         x, y : array-like\n4998 |             The data positions. *x* and *y* must be of the same length.\n4999 | \n5000 |         C : array-like, optional\n5001 |             If given, these values are accumulated in the bins. Otherwise,\n5002 |             every point has a value of 1. Must be of the same length as *x*\n5003 |             and *y*.\n5004 | \n5005 |         gridsize : int or (int, int), default: 100\n5006 |             If a single int, the number of hexagons in the *x*-direction.\n5007 |             The number of hexagons in the *y*-direction is chosen such that\n5008 |             the hexagons are approximately regular.\n5009 | \n5010 |             Alternatively, if a tuple (*nx*, *ny*), the number of hexagons\n5011 |             in the *x*-direction and the *y*-direction. In the\n5012 |             *y*-direction, counting is done along vertically aligned\n5013 |             hexagons, not along the zig-zag chains of hexagons; see the\n5014 |             following illustration.\n5015 | \n5016 |             .. plot::\n5017 | \n5018 |                import numpy\n5019 |                import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n5020 | \n5021 |                np.random.seed(19680801)\n5022 |                n= 300\n5023 |                x = np.random.standard_normal(n)\n5024 |                y = np.random.standard_normal(n)\n5025 | \n5026 |                fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(4, 4))\n5027 |                h = ax.hexbin(x, y, gridsize=(5, 3))\n5028 |                hx, hy = h.get_offsets().T\n5029 |                ax.plot(hx[24::3], hy[24::3], 'ro-')\n5030 |                ax.plot(hx[-3:], hy[-3:], 'ro-')\n5031 |                ax.set_title('gridsize=(5, 3)')\n5032 |                ax.axis('off')\n5033 | \n5034 |             To get approximately regular hexagons, choose\n5035 |             :math:`n_x = \\\\sqrt{3}\\\\,n_y`.\n5036 | \n5037 |         bins : 'log' or int or sequence, default: None\n5038 |             Discretization of the hexagon values.\n5039 | \n5040 |             - If *None*, no binning is applied; the color of each hexagon\n5041 |               directly corresponds to its count value.\n5042 |             - If 'log', use a logarithmic scale for the colormap.\n5043 |               Internally, :math:`log_{10}(i+1)` is used to determine the\n5044 |               hexagon color. This is equivalent to ``norm=LogNorm()``.\n5045 |             - If an integer, divide the counts in the specified number\n5046 |               of bins, and color the hexagons accordingly.\n5047 |             - If a sequence of values, the values of the lower bound of\n5048 |               the bins to be used.\n5049 | \n5050 |         xscale : {'linear', 'log'}, default: 'linear'\n5051 |             Use a linear or log10 scale on the horizontal axis.\n5052 | \n5053 |         yscale : {'linear', 'log'}, default: 'linear'\n5054 |             Use a linear or log10 scale on the vertical axis.\n5055 | \n5056 |         mincnt : int >= 0, default: *None*\n5057 |             If not *None*, only display cells with at least *mincnt*\n5058 |             number of points in the cell.\n5059 | \n5060 |         marginals : bool, default: *False*\n5061 |             If marginals is *True*, plot the marginal density as\n5062 |             colormapped rectangles along the bottom of the x-axis and\n5063 |             left of the y-axis.\n5064 | \n5065 |         extent : 4-tuple of float, default: *None*\n5066 |             The limits of the bins (xmin, xmax, ymin, ymax).\n5067 |             The default assigns the limits based on\n5068 |             *gridsize*, *x*, *y*, *xscale* and *yscale*.\n5069 | \n5070 |             If *xscale* or *yscale* is set to 'log', the limits are\n5071 |             expected to be the exponent for a power of 10. E.g. for\n5072 |             x-limits of 1 and 50 in 'linear' scale and y-limits\n5073 |             of 10 and 1000 in 'log' scale, enter (1, 50, 1, 3).\n5074 | \n5075 |         Returns\n5076 |         -------\n5077 |         `~matplotlib.collections.PolyCollection`\n5078 |             A `.PolyCollection` defining the hexagonal bins.\n5079 | \n5080 |             - `.PolyCollection.get_offsets` contains a Mx2 array containing\n5081 |               the x, y positions of the M hexagon centers in data coordinates.\n5082 |             - `.PolyCollection.get_array` contains the values of the M\n5083 |               hexagons.\n5084 | \n5085 |             If *marginals* is *True*, horizontal\n5086 |             bar and vertical bar (both PolyCollections) will be attached\n5087 |             to the return collection as attributes *hbar* and *vbar*.\n5088 | \n5089 |         Other Parameters\n5090 |         ----------------\n5091 |         %(cmap_doc)s\n5092 | \n5093 |         %(norm_doc)s\n5094 | \n5095 |         %(vmin_vmax_doc)s\n5096 | \n5097 |         alpha : float between 0 and 1, optional\n5098 |             The alpha blending value, between 0 (transparent) and 1 (opaque).\n5099 | \n5100 |         linewidths : float, default: *None*\n5101 |             If *None*, defaults to :rc:`patch.linewidth`.\n5102 | \n5103 |         edgecolors : {'face', 'none', *None*} or color, default: 'face'\n5104 |             The color of the hexagon edges. Possible values are:\n5105 | \n5106 |             - 'face': Draw the edges in the same color as the fill color.\n5107 |             - 'none': No edges are drawn. This can sometimes lead to unsightly\n5108 |               unpainted pixels between the hexagons.\n5109 |             - *None*: Draw outlines in the default color.\n5110 |             - An explicit color.\n5111 | \n5112 |         reduce_C_function : callable, default: `numpy.mean`\n5113 |             The function to aggregate *C* within the bins. It is ignored if\n5114 |             *C* is not given. This must have the signature::\n5115 | \n5116 |                 def reduce_C_function(C: array) -> float\n5117 | \n5118 |             Commonly used functions are:\n5119 | \n5120 |             - `numpy.mean`: average of the points\n5121 |             - `numpy.sum`: integral of the point values\n5122 |             - `numpy.amax`: value taken from the largest point\n5123 | \n5124 |             By default will only reduce cells with at least 1 point because some\n5125 |             reduction functions (such as `numpy.amax`) will error/warn with empty\n5126 |             input. Changing *mincnt* will adjust the cutoff, and if set to 0 will\n5127 |             pass empty input to the reduction function.\n5128 | \n5129 |         %(colorizer_doc)s\n5130 | \n5131 |         data : indexable object, optional\n5132 |             DATA_PARAMETER_PLACEHOLDER\n5133 | \n5134 |         **kwargs : `~matplotlib.collections.PolyCollection` properties\n5135 |             All other keyword arguments are passed on to `.PolyCollection`:\n5136 | \n5137 |             %(PolyCollection:kwdoc)s\n5138 | \n5139 |         See Also\n5140 |         --------\n5141 |         hist2d : 2D histogram rectangular bins\n5142 |         \"\"\"\n5143 |         self._process_unit_info([(\"x\", x), (\"y\", y)], kwargs, convert=False)\n5144 | \n5145 |         x, y, C = cbook.delete_masked_points(x, y, C)\n5146 | \n5147 |         # Set the size of the hexagon grid\n5148 |         if np.iterable(gridsize):\n5149 |             nx, ny = gridsize\n5150 |         else:\n5151 |             nx = gridsize\n5152 |             ny = int(nx / math.sqrt(3))\n5153 |         # Count the number of data in each hexagon\n5154 |         x = np.asarray(x, float)\n5155 |         y = np.asarray(y, float)\n5156 | \n5157 |         # Will be log()'d if necessary, and then rescaled.\n5158 |         tx = x\n5159 |         ty = y\n5160 | \n5161 |         if xscale == 'log':\n5162 |             if np.any(x <= 0.0):\n5163 |                 raise ValueError(\n5164 |                     \"x contains non-positive values, so cannot be log-scaled\")\n5165 |             tx = np.log10(tx)\n5166 |         if yscale == 'log':\n5167 |             if np.any(y <= 0.0):\n5168 |                 raise ValueError(\n5169 |                     \"y contains non-positive values, so cannot be log-scaled\")\n5170 |             ty = np.log10(ty)\n5171 |         if extent is not None:\n5172 |             xmin, xmax, ymin, ymax = extent\n5173 |             if xmin > xmax:\n5174 |                 raise ValueError(\"In extent, xmax must be greater than xmin\")\n5175 |             if ymin > ymax:\n5176 |                 raise ValueError(\"In extent, ymax must be greater than ymin\")\n5177 |         else:\n5178 |             xmin, xmax = (tx.min(), tx.max()) if len(x) else (0, 1)\n5179 |             ymin, ymax = (ty.min(), ty.max()) if len(y) else (0, 1)\n5180 | \n5181 |             # to avoid issues with singular data, expand the min/max pairs\n5182 |             xmin, xmax = mtransforms.nonsingular(xmin, xmax, expander=0.1)\n5183 |             ymin, ymax = mtransforms.nonsingular(ymin, ymax, expander=0.1)\n5184 | \n5185 |         nx1 = nx + 1\n5186 |         ny1 = ny + 1\n5187 |         nx2 = nx\n5188 |         ny2 = ny\n5189 |         n = nx1 * ny1 + nx2 * ny2\n5190 | \n5191 |         # In the x-direction, the hexagons exactly cover the region from\n5192 |         # xmin to xmax. Need some padding to avoid roundoff errors.\n5193 |         padding = 1.e-9 * (xmax - xmin)\n5194 |         xmin -= padding\n5195 |         xmax += padding\n5196 |         sx = (xmax - xmin) / nx\n5197 |         sy = (ymax - ymin) / ny\n5198 |         # Positions in hexagon index coordinates.\n5199 |         ix = (tx - xmin) / sx\n5200 |         iy = (ty - ymin) / sy\n5201 |         ix1 = np.round(ix).astype(int)\n5202 |         iy1 = np.round(iy).astype(int)\n5203 |         ix2 = np.floor(ix).astype(int)\n5204 |         iy2 = np.floor(iy).astype(int)\n5205 |         #